INT. ROOFTOP CALL CENTRE - HOTEL TERRACE - DAY

Forty headsets under striped parasols on the roof of a failing seaside hotel. Seagulls wheel overhead. Customers are reached by phone, by hope, and by the mercy of a hot wind that keeps lifting the scripts from the tables.

ROSALEEN FINNEGAN, fifties, supervisor, walks the rows with a megaphone slung from her shoulder and a thermos of tea. JAMAL TURNER, thirties, team lead, reads a script in a monotone. BIANCA ESTRADA, twenties, newest hire, listens to a caller with her hand over her mouth. KOJO ASARE, forties, quality assurance, sits apart with a stopwatch, a clipboard, and a face like a closed door.

ROSALEEN
(into megaphone)
Remember, people! A smile can be heard down the line!

JAMAL
(not smiling)
Thank you for holding.

Bianca covers the mouthpiece and looks up at Rosaleen, who ignores her. Kojo clicks the stopwatch and writes a figure on his clipboard. A gust flips three scripts off Jamal's table; he chases them across the tiles, snatching, swearing under his breath, while Rosaleen steps neatly over a paper in her path and keeps walking, thermos aloft.

BIANCA
(whispering)
Ms. Finnegan. The customer says his house is on fire.

ROSALEEN
Is it our house?

BIANCA
No.

ROSALEEN
Then follow the script.

BIANCA
But he said--

ROSALEEN
Page four. "I understand your frustration."

Bianca nods. She uncovers the mouthpiece. Kojo clicks the stopwatch again, scratches another figure, and glances at Rosaleen with the faintest lift of one eyebrow. Jamal returns with the scripts clutched to his chest, slaps them on the table, and slumps into his chair.

JAMAL
(low)
Kojo. How many?

KOJO
Forty-one this hour.

JAMAL
Forty-one what?

KOJO
Complaints we failed to resolve.

JAMAL
That's... normal.

KOJO
It was twelve yesterday.

Jamal glances at Rosaleen. She is cheerfully yelling encouragement through the megaphone at a row of headsets. A wave breaks on the beach far below. Bianca pushes her headset off one ear and stares at the horizon.

BIANCA
(very quietly)
Sir? Sir, I'm sorry. I can hear it. I can hear the fire.

She stands. The chair falls over behind her. Jamal rises to catch it. Kojo's stopwatch clicks. Rosaleen turns, megaphone halfway to her mouth.

ROSALEEN
Bianca. Sit.

BIANCA
(into the headset, steady)
Sir, give me your address.

ROSALEEN
Bianca, that is not on the script.

Bianca tears off the headset and drops it on the table. She picks up the phone, the real one, the one with a cord. She dials three digits and holds it to her ear. Jamal steps beside her. Kojo, slowly, sets down his clipboard.

BIANCA
(into phone)
Yes. There's a fire at forty-two Marlow Road.

ROSALEEN
(louder)
Bianca!

Jamal turns to Rosaleen. He takes the megaphone off her shoulder with one hand, unhurried, and sets it on the table.

JAMAL
Rosaleen. Shut up.

The terrace goes silent. Forty heads turn. The wind lifts the scripts and carries them over the parapet, white wings falling into the sea.
